{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975786,"nonce":"j5tZ4CIgZxnqWlwqXdOIJUyctsE=","title":"The GC trigger scales with live size when it should bound GC's share of runtime","message":"`trigger = live_bytes * growth_factor()` with `growth_factor()` = 4. That ties\nthe collection interval to live size, so a program with a small live set and a\nhigh allocation rate (binary_trees: ~12MB live, 900MB allocated) gets a very\nshort interval and many collections, while a program with a large live set and a\nlow allocation rate gets a long one it does not need.\n\nRaising the constant to 8 was measured on the NUC across the whole corpus: two\nrows clear the harness's own 5ms noise floor and both improve (binary_trees\n-7.4%, free_call -5.7%); every other row moves less than 3.4ms absolute. Peak\nRSS rises only on binary_trees, 181 -\u003e 231MB. The reason the max pause does not\nget worse -- which was the stated reason for choosing 4 -- is that pause is 92%\nmarking of the LIVE set, and a longer interval adds garbage, which costs sweep\ntime, not mark time.\n\nBetter than bumping the constant: target a GC time fraction. After each\ncollection compare pause against mutator time since the last one and adjust the\nmultiplier within a clamp, keeping the existing ceiling. binary_trees then gets a\nlonger interval automatically and short-lived programs are untouched, with no\nblanket RSS cost.","files":null}]}